fig(1) - Version Control System for $HOME - multiple Git repositories in $HOME
===============================================================================

## SYNOPSIS

`fig` [<options>] <command>

`fig` add <repo>

`fig` add --all|-a

`fig` clone [-b <branch>] <url> [<repo>]

`fig` bootstrap [--include=<tags>] [--exclude=<tags>] [@<tags>] [<file>]

`fig` clone --all|-a|@<tags> [--include=<tags>] [--exclude=<tags>] [<file>]

`fig` config [<git config arguments>]

`fig` delete <repo>

`fig` enter <repo>

`fig` foreach [-g] <git command>

`fig` help

`fig` init <repo>

`fig` list

`fig` list-tracked [<repo>]

`fig` list-untracked [<-a>] [<-r>] [<repo>]

`fig` pull

`fig` push

`fig` rename <repo> <newname>

`fig` run <repo> <shell command>

`fig` status [<repo>]

`fig` sync [<message>...]

`fig` upgrade <repo>

`fig` version

`fig` which <substring>

`fig` write-gitignore [<repo>]

`fig` <repo> <gitcommand>

`fig` <repo>


## DESCRIPTION

`fig` allows you to have several `git`(1) repositories, all maintaining their
working trees in $HOME without clobbering each other. That, in turn, means you
can have one repository per config set (zsh, vim, ssh, etc), picking and
choosing which configs you want to use on which machine.

`fig` is using a technique called fake bare Git repositories, keeping <$GIT_DIR>
in a different directory from <$GIT_WORK_TREE> which is pointed to <$HOME>.

The use of symlinks is not needed in this setup, making for a cleaner setup.

To set up a new machine, `fig` has a built-in bootstrap. You populate
<$FIG_REPOS> ("<name> <url> <branch> @tags," per repo) with `fig add
<repo> [<tags>]` or `fig add --all`, and track that file in one of your
repos. On the new machine `fig bootstrap` (also `fig clone --all`)
clones everything listed there that is not already present. Give a repo an
optional comma-separated tag list (4th column). An untagged repo is always
cloned; a tagged repo is opt-in and clones only when one of its tags is
given via `fig bootstrap --include=<tags>` (`--exclude=<tags>` always wins).

Please note that you can always use a path instead of a name for <repo>.
This is needed to support external scripts properly and of no concern to
an interactive user.

## OPTIONS

* -c:
  Source <file> prior to other configuration files

* -d:
  Enable debug mode

* -v:
  Enable verbose mode

## COMMANDS

* add:
  Append a repository to <$FIG_REPOS> ("<name> <url> <branch> @tags,"). This
  is the only thing that writes that file.

  `<repo> [<tags>]`: add that repo, with an optional comma-separated tag list
  as a 4th column. Tags are stored with a leading `@` and a trailing `,`
  (`fig add nvim laptop,work` writes `nvim <url> main @laptop,work,`); a
  leading `@` or trailing `,` you pass in is absorbed. The repo must exist
  locally and have an <origin> remote. Already-listed repos are left alone.

  `--all` (or `-a`): add every local repo with an <origin> remote that is not
  already listed (no tags).

* clone:
  Clone an existing repository.

  If you need to clone a bundle of repositories, look into the
  `post-clone-retired` hook.

  You can also use a single git repository with several branches. Use the `-b`
  option to specify a branch at clone time, the default is `master`.

  `--all` (or `-a`, or a bare `@<tags>`): ignore <url>/<repo> and instead
  bootstrap from <$FIG_REPOS> (untagged rows, plus any tagged subset --
  `fig clone @laptop` == `fig bootstrap --include=laptop`). See `bootstrap`
  for the tag rules. This word must come first after `clone`
  (`fig clone --all [<file>]`, `fig clone @laptop`); it is not a normal
  option and cannot be combined with `-b` or a <url>.

* commit:
  Commit in all repositories

* config:
  Edit <$FIG_GITCONFIG>, a git config file that every fig repository
  includes (wired up by `upgrade`). Arguments are passed straight to
  `git config`; with none, the file is listed. Use it for settings that
  should apply to every repo, e.g.

      fig config credential.helper '!gh auth git-credential'
      fig config user.email you@example.com

  Per-repo settings still go through `fig <repo> config ...`.

* delete:
  Delete an existing repository.

* enter:
  Enter repository; spawn new <$SHELL> with <$GIT_DIR> set.

* foreach:
  Execute git command for every fig repository.

  `-g`: Execute in general context.

* help:
  Display help.

* init:
  Initialize an empty repository.

* list:
  List all local fig repositories.

* list-tracked:
  List all files tracked by fig.

  If you want to list files tracked by a specific repository, simply
  append the repository's name last.

* list-tracked-by:
  List files tracked by a repository.

  This is a legacy command; you should use `list-tracked <repo>` instead.

* list-untracked:
  List all files NOT tracked by fig.

  `-a`: Show all files.
  By default, the `git ls-files --exclude-standard` is called.

  `-r`: Recursive mode.
  By default, the file list is shallow and stops at directory levels where
  possible.

  `$repo`: List files not tracked by this specific repository.

* pull:
  Pull from all fig remotes.

* push:
  Push to all fig remotes.

* bootstrap:
  Read <$FIG_REPOS> and run `fig clone` for every listed repository that
  is not already present locally. Existing repos are left untouched. The forms
  `fig bootstrap`, `fig clone --all` and `fig clone -a` are equivalent.

  A row with no 4th-column tags is always cloned. A row that has tags is
  opt-in: it is cloned only when `--include` names one of its tags, so a
  bare `fig bootstrap` clones the untagged rows only.

  `--include=<tags>`: comma-separated. Also clone tagged rows that carry one
  of these tags.

  `@<tags>`: a bare positional starting with `@` (e.g. `fig bootstrap @laptop`
  or `@laptop,work`) is shorthand for `--include=<tags>`. Repeated
  `--include` / `--exclude` / `@<tags>` arguments accumulate.

  `--exclude=<tags>`: comma-separated. Skip any row that has one of these tags.
  Exclude wins over include. Matching is case-insensitive, and a leading `@`
  on any tag you pass is optional (`--include=@laptop` == `--include=laptop`).

  `<file>`: read from this path instead of <$FIG_REPOS>. A file argument is
  told apart from `@<tags>` by the leading `@`.

* rename:
  Rename a repository.

* run:
  Run command with <$GIT_DIR> and <$GIT_WORK_TREE> set. Allows you to run any
  and all commands without any restrictions. Use with care.

  Please note that there is a somewhat magic feature for run. Instead of <repo>
  it accepts <path>, as well. Anything that has a slash in it will be assumed to
  be a path. `fig run` will then operate on this directory instead of the one
  normally generated from the repository's name.
  This is needed to support external scripts properly and of no concern to
  an interactive user.

* status:
  Show statuses of all/one fig repositories.

* sync:
  `git add -u`, commit, and push every repo in one step. Equivalent to
  running `foreach add -u`, `commit -m <message>`, and `push` in sequence.

  `[<message>...]`: the commit message; multiple words are joined with
  spaces, so quoting is optional. Defaults to "update".

  Prints "Adding...", "Committing...", and "Pushing..." phase labels, but
  only when there is more than one repo -- with a single repo there's
  nothing to disambiguate, same rule as the per-repo label in `commit`,
  `pull`, `push`, and `foreach`.

* upgrade:
  Upgrade repository to currently recommended settings.

* version:
  Print version information.

* which <substring>:
  Find <substring> in name of any tracked file.

* write-gitignore:
  Write <repo>.config/.gitignore via `git ls-files`. If <repo> is not specified
  but we're within a fig repository, use that.

* <repo> <gitcommand>:
  Shortcut to run `git` commands on a repo. Will prepend `git` to <gitcommand>.

* <repo>:
  Shortcut to run `fig enter <repo>`.

## ENVIRONMENT

As noted earlier, `fig` will set <$GIT_DIR> and <$GIT_WORK_TREE> to the
appropriate values for fake bare Git repositories.

## CONFIG

There are several ways to turn the various knobs on `fig`. In order of
ascending precedence, they are:

* `VARIABLE=foo fig`
* </etc/fig/figrc>
* <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.figrc>
* <$FIG_REPO_D/<repo>.config/.figrc> (only when acting on <repo>)
* `fig -c <file>`

Please note that those files are sourced. Any and all commands will be
executed in the context of your shell.

The per-repo file <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/<repo>.config/.figrc> is read only
while operating on that repository. It lives inside the <repo>.config/
directory (next to <repo>.git/), which `rename` moves and `delete` removes as
a whole.

Separately from those shell files, `upgrade` points every repository's
`include.path` at <$FIG_GITCONFIG>
(<$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.gitconfig>). Put git settings that should apply
to all repositories there -- `credential.helper`, `user.name`, `user.email`,
and so on -- via the `config` command. `upgrade` also sets
`status.showUntrackedFiles` in each repo (see <$FIG_SHOW_UNTRACKED>) and
`core.excludesfile` / `core.attributesfile` (see <$FIG_GITIGNORE_FILE>).

Because the shared fallback ignore file is a single `*` (so `git add .` in a
fig repo does not vacuum up all of `$HOME`), `fig <repo> add <path> ...`
supplies `-f` for you when the catch-all `*` is the only thing ignoring the
named paths. If any path is matched by a more specific pattern -- a
hand-written rule in a <repo>.config/.gitignore, `.git/info/exclude`, etc. --
`fig` does not force, so git's normal "use -f" refusal still protects a
deliberate ignore. The implicit `-f` is also skipped for bulk forms (`.`,
`-A`, `--all`, `-u`, `--update`, `-n`, `--dry-run`, ...), when you pass
`-f`/`--force` yourself, and when <$FIG_ADD_FORCE> is <no>.

Interesting knobs you can turn:

* <$FIG_GITCONFIG>:
  Path of the shared git config file included by every repository.

  Defaults to <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.gitconfig>.

* <$FIG_ADD_FORCE>:
  Set to <no> to disable the implicit `-f` on `fig <repo> add` described
  above, so `add` behaves exactly like `git add`.

  Defaults to unset (implicit `-f` enabled).

* <$FIG_GITIGNORE_FILE>, <$FIG_GITATTRIBUTES_FILE>:
  Shared fallback ignore / attributes files, used by a repo when it has no
  <repo>.config/.gitignore (resp. gitattributes) of its own.

  Default to <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.gitignore> and
  <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.gitattributes>.

* <$FIG_SHOW_UNTRACKED>:
  Value written to `status.showUntrackedFiles` in each repo by `upgrade`.

  Defaults to <no>, so `git status` in a fig repo ignores the rest of
  `$HOME`. Set to <normal> (or override per repo) to see untracked files.

* <$FIG_GITATTRIBUTES>:
  Can be <none>, or any other value.

  <none> will not maintain Git attributes in a special location.

  If set to any other value, repo-specific gitattributes files will be maintained.

  Defaults to <none>.

* <$FIG_GITIGNORE>:
  Can be <exact>, <none>, or <recursive>.

  <exact> will seed the repo-specific ignore file with all file and directory
  names which `git ls-files` returns.

  <none> will not write any ignore file.

  <recursive> will descend through all directories recursively additionally to
  the above.

  Defaults to <exact>.

* <$FIG_WORKTREE>:
  Can be <absolute>, or <relative>.

  <absolute> will set an absolute path; defaulting to <$HOME>.

  <relative> will set a path relative to <$GIT_DIR>.

  Defaults to <absolute>.

Less interesting knobs you could turn:

* <$FIG_DEBUG>:
  Enter debug mode.

* <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME>:
  As specified in the 'XDG Base Directory Specification', see
  <http://standards.freedesktop.org/basedir-spec/basedir-spec-latest.html>

  Defaults to <$HOME/.config>.

* <$FIG_REPO_D>:
  The directory where repositories are read from and stored.

  Defaults to <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig>.

* <$FIG_REPOS>:
  The repo list you build with `fig add` and `bootstrap` reads
  ("<name> <url> <branch> @tags," per repo). `fig` never writes it on its own --
  `delete` and `rename` leave stale lines for you to edit out.

  Defaults to <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/.repos>.

* <$FIG_HOOK_D>:
  The directory where hooks are read from.

  Defaults to <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/hooks>.

* <$FIG_BASE>:
  The directory where repositories are checked out to.

  Defaults to <$HOME>.


## HOOK SYSTEM

`fig` provides a hook system. Hook scripts must be executable and placed in
<$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/hooks> (or wherever <$FIG_HOOK_D> points); `fig`
runs every executable hook it finds there.

Hooks follow a simple format. <pre-run> will be run before anything is run.
If you want to have more than one script for a certain hook, just append
any kind of string to order them. A system of <pre-run>, <pre-run\.10>,
<pre-run\.20> etc is suggested; other options would be <pre-run-10> or
<pre-run\.sh>. A dot after the hook name is optional.

If you want to create hooks for a specific <fig> repository, simply prepend
the repository's name, followed by a dot, i.e. <zsh\.pre-run>. Otherwise, the
same rules as above apply. The dot between the repository's name and the hook
is mandatory, though.

Available hooks are <pre-clone>, <post-clone>, <post-clone-retired>,
<pre-command>, <post-command>, <pre-enter>, <post-enter>, <pre-init>,
<post-init>, <pre-pull>, <post-pull>, <pre-push>, <post-push>, <pre-run>,
<post-run>, <pre-upgrade>, and <post-upgrade>.
If you need more, fig is trivial to patch, but please let upstream know so
we can ship them by default.

## OVERLAY SYSTEM

`fig` also provides an overlay system. Similar to hooks, overlay scripts go
in <$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fig/overlays> (or wherever <$FIG_OVERLAY_D> points).

Overlays follow the same rules as hooks and you are free to overwrite any
and all functions. Same as hooks, you can use global or repository-specific
overlays by using either <$FIG_OVERLAY_D/$FIG_COMMAND> or
<$FIG_OVERLAY_D/$FIG_REPO_NAME.$FIG_COMMAND>.

Please note that nothing stops you from, e.g. overwriting `status()` in
<$FIG_OVERLAY_D/commit>. As the overlays will be sourced and you are
replacing arbitrary functions, any and all features may stop working, or you
may even lose data.

You have been warned.

## DETAILED HOWTO AND FURTHER READING

Manpages are often short and sometimes useless to glean best practices from.
While the author tried to avoid this in this case, manpages can not cover
detailed howtos.

This software also comes with a file called <README\.md>. It contains various
approaches to setting up and using fig. You can view the file it as
plain text or render it into various other formats via Markdown.

On Debian-based systems, this file can be found in </usr/share/doc/fig>.

## SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS

`fig` allows you to execute arbitrary commands via `fig run`. For example,
adding a `sudo`(8) rule for `fig` would be pretty stupid.

Additionally, fig will source, i.e. execute, all files listed in <CONFIG>.
You can put any and all commands into these config files and they will be
executed.

## BUGS

None are known at this time, but reports and/or patches are more than welcome.

## INTEROPERABILITY

If you rely on `git submodule` use `git` 1.7.12 or later. Earlier versions
do not clean internal variables properly before descending into submodules,
resulting in unhappy end users.

## HISTORY

Like most people, the author initially made do with a single repository for all
config files, all of which were soft-linked into <$HOME>.

Martin F. Krafft aka madduck came up with the concept of fake bare Git
repositories.

fig was initially written by madduck. This version is a re-implementation from
scratch with a lot more features. madduck graciously agreed to let the author
take over the name.

